 der-nemerov-the-work-of-helen-frankenthaler\n\nWhat is the relationship be
 tween an artwork and its maker? Can we confidently link the two\, or do th
 ey diverge in meaning and intention? Join us for a keynote lecture by Stan
 ford Art History Professor Alexander Nemerov\, author of Fierce Poise: Hel
 en Frankenthaler and 1950s New York\, as he reflects on these enduring que
 stions. Professor Nemerov will focus on the prints of Helen Frankenthaler\
 , which were recently gifted to the Cantor Arts Center by the Helen Franke
 nthaler Foundation as part of a major exhibition on the artist’s work\, 
 to explore her groundbreaking legacy.\n\n​RSVP here.\n\nAbout the Speake
 r:\n\nAlexander Nemerov\, the Carl and Marilynn Thoma Professor in the Art
 s and Humanities at Stanford\, is the author of many books\, including mos
 t recently Fierce Poise: Helen Frankenthaler and 1950s New York\, and anot
 her book called The Forest: A Fable of America in the 1830s\, which the no
 velist Annie Proulx called “one of the richest books ever to come my way
 \, achingly painful and astonishingly tender.” At Stanford\, Nemerov tea
 ches a popular course called “How to Look at Art and Why."\n\nAll public
  programs at the Cantor Arts Center are always free! Space for this progra
